      {
        "id": "s2_resolve",
        "kind": "equation",
        "label": "Resolving the weight",
        "marks": 2,
        "mark_note": "Resolving",
        "lines": [
          "A bob of mass m hangs by a light inextensible",
          "string of length L from a rigid support S.",
          "Pull it aside through a small angle θ and",
          "release it. At A its weight m g splits into",
          "two perpendicular parts:",
          {
            "text": "m g cos θ — balanced by the tension T",
            "style": "eq"
          },
          {
            "text": "m g sin θ — along the arc, unbalanced",
            "style": "eq"
          }
        ],
        "why": "Only the unbalanced part can accelerate the bob, so mg sin θ is the whole of the restoring force.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Swapping sine and cosine — the component ALONG the string is the cosine one."
        ],
        "recall": {
          "credit": "say_it",
          "must_convey": "That mg splits into mg cos theta balanced by the tension and mg sin theta along the arc.",
          "accept": [
            "mg cos theta balanced by tension",
            "mg sin theta is the restoring force"
          ],
          "reject": [
            "sin theta is nearly theta"
          ],
          "heard_as": [
            "mg cos theta",
            "mg sin theta"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "s3_small_angle",
        "kind": "equation",
        "label": "The small-angle step",
        "marks": 2,
        "mark_note": "Small angle",
        "lines": [
          "The restoring force acts back towards O, so",
          {
            "text": "F = −m g sin θ",
            "style": "eq"
          },
          "For a SMALL angle, sin θ ≈ θ, and from the",
          "figure θ = x/L. With F = m a,",
          {
            "text": "m a = −m g (x/L)   ⇒   a = −(g/L) x",
            "style": "eq"
          },
          "a ∝ −x, so the motion is simple harmonic."
        ],
        "why": "The small-angle step is where the approximation lives — a pendulum is only ACCURATELY simple harmonic for small swings, and saying so is honest physics.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Using sin θ ≈ θ with θ in degrees; the approximation holds only in radians."
        ],
        "recall": {
          "credit": "say_it",
          "must_convey": "That F is minus mg sin theta, sin theta is nearly theta which is x by L, giving a equals minus g by L times x.",
          "accept": [
            "sin theta is nearly theta",
            "theta is x by L",
            "a equals minus g by L into x"
          ],
          "reject": [
            "mg cos theta balanced by tension"
          ],
          "heard_as": [
            "small angle",
            "g by L"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "s4_period",
        "kind": "equation",
        "label": "The time period",
        "marks": 2,
        "mark_note": "Time period",
        "lines": [
          "Comparing a = −(g/L)x with a = −ω²x,",
          {
            "text": "ω² = g/L   ⇒   ω = √(g/L)",
            "style": "eq"
          },
          {
            "text": "T = 2π/ω = 2π√(L/g)",
            "style": "eq"
          },
          "The period depends only on L and g — never on",
          "the mass of the bob, and not on the amplitude",
          "so long as the angle stays small."
        ],
        "why": "That the mass cancels is the famous part: a heavy bob and a light bob on the same string keep the same time.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing T = 2π√(g/L) with the fraction upside down."
        ],
        "recall": {
          "credit": "say_it",
          "must_convey": "That omega is root g by L, so T is 2 pi root L by g, independent of the mass.",
          "accept": [
            "T is 2 pi root L by g",
            "omega is root g by L",
            "independent of the mass"
          ],
          "reject": [
            "sin theta is nearly theta"
          ],
          "heard_as": [
            "2 pi root L by g",
            "mass cancels"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "s5_seconds",
        "kind": "boxed_final",
        "label": "Seconds pendulum",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Seconds pendulum",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "Seconds pendulum: T = 2 s",
            "style": "boxed"
          },
          "A simple pendulum whose time period of",
          "oscillation is 2 seconds is called a seconds",
          "pendulum. On the surface of the earth its",
          "length works out to about 1 metre."
        ],
        "why": "Two seconds per full swing means one second per half swing — which is why the length was chosen for clocks.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Saying its time period is 1 second; it is 2 seconds, one for each half swing."
        ],
        "recall": {
          "credit": "say_it",
